11	// These tests pin the exact-readback rule for the owning-circuit value on a
12	// saved proof job — the same rule the job id and a circuit's own name already
13	// carry. The "circuit" string a job saves is the sole authority for which
14	// frozen version, trusted setup and compiled artifact the job belongs to, so
15	// its raw bytes must denote that circuit exactly.
16	//
17	// encoding/json silently rewrites two damage shapes to U+FFFD:
18	//   - invalid UTF-8 bytes in the literal portions of the token, and
19	//   - \uXXXX escapes forming an unpaired surrogate.
20	//
21	// Without the rule a job saved with such a token could, on read, collide
22	// with a genuinely different circuit whose name contains "�" — and when that
23	// version is frozen, has a trusted setup and a compiled artifact, the job
24	// would keep succeeding against the wrong circuit. The read must instead be
25	// refused as data corruption: ErrDataCorrupt naming the job (by its id when
26	// legible, else by record position) and the circuit field, no partial
27	// results, the file kept byte-for-byte, and the failure never reported as
28	// "unknown circuit" or "missing setup". The rule covers the canonical
29	// "circuit", ASCII letter-case variants and JSON-escaped spellings of the
30	// key. Direct Chinese, emoji, an actually committed "�", a legal surrogate
31	// pair and the literal six-character text \uD800 all stay ordinary values
32	// matched exactly.
